WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.630
This is the last part of respiratory drugs: dosing and delivery.

2
00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:19.819
We start with the standard dosing abbreviations, and each drug's site of action and usual frequency, grouped as bronchodilators, controllers, and mucolytics and anti-infectives.

3
00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:30.080
Then the order to give several inhaled drugs in, how to pick the drug and the device, and how to nebulize through CPAP or BiPAP.

4
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:37.375
We finish with drugs given down the endotracheal tube: the emergency drugs, and surfactant.

5
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.837
First the abbreviations, from Hess.

6
00:00:41.837 --> 00:00:54.595
Q D is daily, B I D twice a day, T I D three times a day, Q I D four times a day, P R N as needed, and q four h or q six h every four or six hours.

7
00:00:54.595 --> 00:01:01.104
Hess adds that drugs with longer response times typically need fewer doses.

8
00:01:01.104 --> 00:01:12.125
Then the receptor map: beta one is the heart, beta two the lungs, alpha one vasoconstriction, and M three airway smooth muscle.

9
00:01:12.125 --> 00:01:18.721
Albuterol and levalbuterol, the short-acting beta agonists, act on beta two.

10
00:01:18.721 --> 00:01:27.573
Hess gives albuterol as needed, every four to six hours; the deck gives no frequency for levalbuterol.

11
00:01:27.573 --> 00:01:42.500
Racemic epinephrine is strongly alpha adrenergic, given as two point two five percent, point two five to point five milliliters by small volume nebulizer, four times a day.

12
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:57.555
Long-acting beta agonists: salmeterol, fifty micrograms per puff by dry powder inhaler, twice a day; and formoterol, twenty micrograms by nebulizer, twice a day.

13
00:01:57.555 --> 00:02:03.985
The ultra long-acting indacaterol, olodaterol and vilanterol are once daily.

14
00:02:03.985 --> 00:02:17.691
Among the anticholinergics, ipratropium blocks muscarinic receptors with no subtype specificity and is given four times a day per the deck; Hess says two to four.

15
00:02:17.691 --> 00:02:23.444
Combivent Respimat, ipratropium plus albuterol, is four times a day.

16
00:02:23.444 --> 00:02:30.551
Tiotropium is selective for M one and M three and is once daily, as is umeclidinium.

17
00:02:30.551 --> 00:02:39.350
Aclidinium, which acts on all muscarinic receptors, is twice a day, and so is glycopyrrolate, or Seebri.

18
00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:52.633
Theophylline is twice daily as an oral sustained-release tablet; its exact mechanism is unknown, though phosphodiesterase inhibition is most commonly taught.

19
00:02:54.067 --> 00:03:07.846
Inhaled corticosteroids, such as beclomethasone, act on intracellular receptors and are given once or twice a day, per Hess; Heuer gives QVAR two puffs twice daily.

20
00:03:07.846 --> 00:03:13.308
Cromolyn, a mast cell stabilizer, is four times daily, per Heuer.

21
00:03:13.308 --> 00:03:19.357
Montelukast blocks the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor and is once daily.

22
00:03:19.357 --> 00:03:29.104
Zafirlukast is ten milligrams twice a day for ages five to eleven, and twenty milligrams twice a day from twelve up.

23
00:03:29.104 --> 00:03:36.582
Zileuton blocks the five lipoxygenase enzyme, at six hundred milligrams four times a day.

24
00:03:36.582 --> 00:03:42.884
Omalizumab targets IgE and is given subcutaneously every two to four weeks.

25
00:03:42.884 --> 00:04:06.242
The anti interleukin five antibodies: mepolizumab, one hundred milligrams subcutaneously every four weeks; reslizumab, three milligrams per kilogram IV every four weeks; and benralizumab, thirty milligrams subcutaneously every four weeks for three doses, then every eight weeks.

26
00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:17.863
Acetylcysteine breaks mucus disulfide bonds and is given three to four times daily by small volume nebulizer, per Heuer.

27
00:04:17.863 --> 00:04:24.321
Dornase alfa breaks down DNA in secretions: two point five milligrams daily.

28
00:04:24.321 --> 00:04:38.596
Tobramycin, or TOBI, acts on the thirty S ribosomal subunit to block protein synthesis: three hundred milligrams twice a day, twenty eight days on and twenty eight off.

29
00:04:38.596 --> 00:04:51.682
Aztreonam, or Cayston, inhibits cell wall synthesis: three times a day, twenty eight days on and twenty eight off, with a bronchodilator before each dose.

30
00:04:51.682 --> 00:04:56.950
Pentamidine is three hundred milligrams once every four weeks.

31
00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:05.192
Palivizumab targets the RSV F protein: fifteen milligrams per kilogram IM, monthly in RSV season.

32
00:05:05.192 --> 00:05:11.650
Alpha one proteinase inhibitor is sixty milligrams per kilogram once weekly.

33
00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:22.017
No frequency is given for the paralytics or sedatives, and opioids are given as needed or by patient-controlled analgesia.

34
00:05:23.433 --> 00:05:25.207
Pause and sort them.

35
00:05:25.207 --> 00:05:30.881
Tiotropium, ipratropium and aclidinium are all anticholinergics.

36
00:05:30.881 --> 00:05:37.708
Which one is given once daily, which four times a day, and which twice a day?

37
00:05:38.633 --> 00:05:44.633
[6-second practice pause]

38
00:05:45.133 --> 00:05:52.461
Tiotropium, the long-acting drug selective for M one and M three, is once daily.

39
00:05:52.461 --> 00:05:58.690
Ipratropium, the short-acting one, is four times a day per the deck.

40
00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:01.071
Aclidinium is twice a day.

41
00:06:01.071 --> 00:06:07.483
Umeclidinium, another long-acting anticholinergic, is also once daily.

42
00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:15.984
If the patient is receiving several inhaled medications, the deck gives a numbered order.

43
00:06:15.984 --> 00:06:26.412
First the bronchodilator, then the mucolytic, then airway clearance therapy, then steroids, and finally the aerosolized antibiotic.

44
00:06:26.412 --> 00:06:43.685
The logic runs straight down the list: open the airway, thin the secretions, get them out, then put the anti-inflammatory and the antibiotic into an airway that is open and clear, so they reach lung rather than mucus.

45
00:06:43.685 --> 00:06:49.177
The deck states the rule twice: always give the bronchodilator first.

46
00:06:49.177 --> 00:07:03.425
All mucokinetics irritate the airway, so precede them with a bronchodilator to prevent bronchospasm, and pair them with airway clearance so the loosened secretions actually leave.

47
00:07:04.833 --> 00:07:06.275
Picking the drug.

48
00:07:06.275 --> 00:07:19.416
All beta agonists have some cardiovascular and CNS effects, so recommend those with the least beta one and most beta two effect: albuterol or levalbuterol.

49
00:07:19.416 --> 00:07:27.471
Short-acting beta agonists relieve bronchospasm; long-acting ones and steroids are controllers.

50
00:07:27.471 --> 00:07:38.493
In an acute asthma exacerbation, repeat the standard dose every twenty minutes up to three times, or give continuous nebulization.

51
00:07:38.493 --> 00:07:55.959
Long-acting beta agonists may increase the risk of severe exacerbations or death in some asthmatics by masking inflammation, so pair them with an inhaled steroid, and never use them for acute exacerbations.

52
00:07:55.959 --> 00:08:02.572
For COPD maintenance: salmeterol, tiotropium, or umeclidinium with vilanterol.

53
00:08:02.572 --> 00:08:09.355
Aerosolized antibiotics are generally only for cystic fibrosis with Pseudomonas.

54
00:08:09.355 --> 00:08:21.733
Inhaled steroids are first-line for mild persistent asthma; except for budesonide, they come as MDIs or DPIs, so rinse the mouth and use a spacer.

55
00:08:23.167 --> 00:08:27.856
Picking the device follows the deck's logic, in order.

56
00:08:27.856 --> 00:08:35.757
First, check the available formulations; some drugs come only one way, and that decides it.

57
00:08:35.757 --> 00:08:43.312
Reserve a small volume nebulizer for acutely ill adults who cannot use a DPI or an MDI.

58
00:08:43.312 --> 00:08:53.645
Most infants and small children should get an MDI with a holding chamber and mask, with a nebulizer as the alternative.

59
00:08:53.645 --> 00:09:04.847
An adult who is not acutely ill but cannot coordinate actuation with breathing needs a holding chamber, or a breath-actuated MDI.

60
00:09:04.847 --> 00:09:15.788
DPIs are ideal for maintenance in outpatient adults, larger children and adolescents who can generate enough inspiratory flow.

61
00:09:15.788 --> 00:09:20.216
If several devices qualify, let the patient choose.

62
00:09:20.216 --> 00:09:34.370
To nebulize through CPAP or BiPAP, put the adaptor near the mask, and match the driving gas to the delivered FiO2; on a high FiO2, drive the nebulizer with oxygen.

63
00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:41.317
For neonates and infants, use a blender and monitor pulse oximetry continuously.

64
00:09:42.733 --> 00:09:44.670
Pause and order them.

65
00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:55.275
A cystic fibrosis patient is due for tobramycin, budesonide, dornase alfa, albuterol, and airway clearance therapy.

66
00:09:55.275 --> 00:09:58.133
In what order do you give them?

67
00:09:59.033 --> 00:10:05.033
[6-second practice pause]

68
00:10:05.533 --> 00:10:08.910
Albuterol first, to open the airway.

69
00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:12.850
Then dornase alfa, to thin the secretions.

70
00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:16.509
Then airway clearance, to get them out.

71
00:10:16.509 --> 00:10:19.229
Then budesonide, the steroid.

72
00:10:19.229 --> 00:10:26.358
And last, tobramycin, the antibiotic, into an airway that is open and clear.

73
00:10:27.767 --> 00:10:44.350
Selected drugs can be instilled as liquids directly into the lungs through a tracheal airway, and the NBRC cares about two situations: emergency drugs when there is no IV access, and surfactant.

74
00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:53.838
The emergency drugs spell LEAN: lidocaine, epinephrine, atropine and naloxone; some add vasopressin, for NAVEL.

75
00:10:53.838 --> 00:10:55.890
Five rules go with them.

76
00:10:55.890 --> 00:11:00.762
The dose must be two to two and a half times the IV dose.

77
00:11:00.762 --> 00:11:06.917
Dilute it with ten milliliters of sterile water or saline for injection.

78
00:11:06.917 --> 00:11:10.507
Put the patient supine, not Trendelenburg.

79
00:11:10.507 --> 00:11:18.627
Halt chest compressions, and instill through a catheter that passes beyond the tip of the tube.

80
00:11:18.627 --> 00:11:26.492
Immediately afterward, give five to ten rapid inflations with a bag-valve-mask resuscitator.

81
00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:37.879
Surfactant is given prophylactically to infants at high risk of RDS, or as rescue for infants with clinical evidence of it.

82
00:11:37.879 --> 00:11:45.019
Recommend a chest film first to confirm tube position, and suction beforehand if needed.

83
00:11:45.019 --> 00:11:48.264
Monitor saturation and ECG continuously.

84
00:11:48.264 --> 00:11:56.539
Use a five French feeding tube or suction catheter, inserted up to, but not past, the tip of the tube.

85
00:11:56.539 --> 00:12:03.030
Note the contrast: the emergency drugs go past the tip, but surfactant does not.

86
00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:13.820
Split the dose in half, and instill half into each dependent bronchus, turning the infant from side to side, as rapidly as tolerated.

87
00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:18.039
Manually ventilate for one to two minutes afterward.

88
00:12:18.039 --> 00:12:31.750
Watch blood gases and chest movement closely for the first three hours, because the infant may briefly need more support, and avoid suctioning for six hours if possible.

89
00:12:33.167 --> 00:12:36.646
The care plan deck adds when to choose it.

90
00:12:36.646 --> 00:12:46.173
Recommend exogenous surfactant for a premature newborn having difficulty breathing, or with clinical signs of IRDS.

91
00:12:46.173 --> 00:13:03.072
Recommend prophylactic rather than rescue administration, especially below thirty weeks' gestation, because it decreases the risk of pneumothorax, pulmonary interstitial emphysema, and neonatal mortality.

92
00:13:03.072 --> 00:13:09.534
Recommend multiple doses if required; they have advantages over a single dose.

93
00:13:09.534 --> 00:13:17.984
The agents named are beractant, or Survanta; calfactant, or Infasurf; and poractant alfa, or Curosurf.

94
00:13:17.984 --> 00:13:22.292
Their doses per kilogram are covered in chapter two.

95
00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:24.619
Last one.

96
00:13:24.619 --> 00:13:29.829
During a code, an intubated adult has no IV access.

97
00:13:29.829 --> 00:13:35.141
Suppose the IV dose of epinephrine is one milligram.

98
00:13:35.141 --> 00:13:39.125
Can you give it down the tube, and how?

99
00:13:40.033 --> 00:13:46.033
[6-second practice pause]

100
00:13:46.533 --> 00:13:47.133
Yes.

101
00:13:47.133 --> 00:13:50.231
Epinephrine is one of the LEAN drugs.

102
00:13:50.231 --> 00:14:01.868
Give two to two and a half times the IV dose, so two to two and a half milligrams, diluted with ten milliliters of sterile water or saline.

103
00:14:01.868 --> 00:14:15.583
Keep the patient supine, pause compressions, instill through a catheter that passes beyond the tube's tip, and follow with five to ten rapid bag-valve-mask inflations.

104
00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:19.319
Here is part three in brief.

105
00:14:19.319 --> 00:14:31.327
Know the abbreviations, Q D through Q I D, and each drug's site of action: beta two for the bronchodilators, muscarinic for the anticholinergics.

106
00:14:31.327 --> 00:14:36.213
Ipratropium is four times a day, and tiotropium once daily.

107
00:14:36.213 --> 00:14:44.412
Give inhaled drugs in order: bronchodilator, mucolytic, airway clearance, steroid, then antibiotic.

108
00:14:44.412 --> 00:14:56.420
Choose the drug with the most beta two and least beta one effect, and pick the device by formulation, illness, coordination and inspiratory flow.

109
00:14:56.420 --> 00:15:10.250
Down the tube, the LEAN drugs go at two to two and a half times the IV dose in ten milliliters, and surfactant goes in two halves, to but not past the tip of the tube.
